

  <section xml:id="Math2301Day20">
    <title>Treeson</title>


    <p>
      Recall that a <term>graph</term> is a network with a collection of vertices <m>V</m> and edges <m>E</m> given as a set of pairs of vertices.
      This week we will discuss a few ways to build a matrix from a graph and how to relate properties of that graph to properties of the matrix.
    </p>

    <p>
      Recall that <m>e_{i}</m> denotes the vector with <m>1</m> in the <m>i</m>th coordinate and zeroes otherwise.
      The <term>signed incidence matrix</term>: if two nodes <m>i \lt j</m> are connected, add a column vector <m>e_{i} - e_{j}</m>.
      Note that the order of the column vectors does not really matter for modeling purposes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Use the following computational tool <url href="https://alexblackmath.github.io/Math2000/Simulations/SignedIncidenceMatrices">on this site</url> to help answer the following questions:
    </p>

    <!-- and <url href="https://alexblackmath.github.io/Math2000/Simulations/UnsignedIncidenceMatrices">on this site</url> -->

    <exercise xml:id="day20-cycles">
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Start with networks that are just a cycle.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Characterize what the signed incidence matrices look like.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            When are their columns independent, if ever? Explain your answer.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="day20-add-edge-dependent">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why adding an edge to a network for which the columns of its signed incidence matrix are dependent can never make it independent.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="day20-five-vertices">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Characterize which networks on at most 5 vertices are independent.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="day20-add-edge-independent">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Take an independent network and add an edge between two nodes.
          When does this stop the columns of the signed incidence matrix of the network from being independent and why?
          When does it preserve independence?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="day20-when-independent">
      <statement>
        <p>
          When are the columns of such a matrix independent?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="day20-never-spanning">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Prove that the columns are never spanning.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="day20-max-rank">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the maximum rank of such a matrix? When is the maximum attained?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="day20-independent-max-rank">
      <statement>
        <p>
          When are the columns both independent and the rank maximal?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="day20-laplacian">
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Let <m>B</m> be such an incidence matrix.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that
            <me>
              q_{BB^{\intercal}}(\vec{x}) = \sum_{(i,j) \in E} (x_{i}-x_{j})^{2}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that <m>BB^{\intercal}</m> is positive semidefinite.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that <m>BB^{\intercal} = D - A</m>, where <m>D</m> is the diagonal matrix with <m>D_{i,i}</m> equal to the number of vertices adjacent to vertex <m>i</m> and <m>A_{i,j} = 1</m> if <m>i</m> and <m>j</m> are adjacent and <m>0</m> otherwise.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>

  </section>

